
 You are an expert iOS developer using Swift and SwiftUI. Follow these guidelines:


  # Product / MLP Vision (Mindset Ritual App)
  - **What we're building:** Gratitude + mindset journaling meets Duolingo — gamified, habit-forming, fun.
  - **Psychology basis:** Positive Psychology, Stoic, CBT, evidence-based daily exercises.
  - **Curated prompts (quiz-driven):** Gratitude, memento mori, goal setting, what went well, "what did you forget to give yourself credit for?", etc.
  - **Gamification:** Daily streak, XP, archetype building, weekly summaries, AI feedback, animations, haptics, sound effects. Progress that motivates.
  - **Future:** XP → Zen garden or visual progress representation on Dashboard. See `Context.md` for full MLP roadmap.
  - **Onboarding flow (14 steps):** Intro → Quiz (5Q) → Analyzing → Archetype Reveal → Pain Screens (3) → How App Helps → Social Proof → AI Coach Intro → Custom Plan → Sign in with Apple → Paywall → App. See `Context.md` §4 for complete flow.


  # Swift & iOS Rules

  # Modularization Rules
  - Business logic belongs in `Domain`.
  - API and Persistence belong in `Data`.
  - Views belong in `Feature[Name]` modules.
  - Never import a Feature module into another Feature module (use the Coordinator).
  - Feature modules: FeatureDashboard, FeatureHistory, FeatureMindset, FeatureOnboarding, FeatureAuth, FeatureSubscription.
  - FeatureAuth handles Sign in with Apple via Firebase Auth; never implement auth in other Feature modules.

  
  # AI Rules
  - Use `gemini-2.0-flash` for all AIService calls.
  - Ensure API keys are never hardcoded; suggest `Secrets.plist` or Environment Variables.

  
 # Auth & Data Sync Rules
 - Use Firebase Auth for Sign in with Apple (FeatureAuth module).
 - Firebase UID is the single source of truth for user identity.
 - SwiftData is local-first (offline support); Firestore is cloud backup.
 - Sync on auth + background refresh; last-write-wins for conflicts.
 - Never hardcode Firebase config; use gitignored `GoogleService-Info.plist`.
 - Services: `FirebaseAuthService`, `FirebaseSyncService` in Data module.
 - RevenueCat user ID should match Firebase UID for subscription tracking.

 
 # Service Factory Pattern (Debug vs Production)
 - **ALWAYS use ServiceFactory** to create services and repositories in `MindsetApp.swift`.
 - **Never hardcode service instantiation** with scattered `#if DEBUG` blocks.
 - **Default behavior:** `ServiceFactory(config: .default)` uses Mock services in Debug builds, Real services in Release builds.
 - **Override for testing:** Use `ServiceFactory(config: .production)` to test real backends in Debug mode.
 - **Benefits:** Clean separation, type-safe, performant (Firebase skipped in mocks), testable, secure (no API keys in mocks).
 - **Mock services:** Instant responses, pre-seeded data, no network calls, perfect for UI development and SwiftUI previews.
 - **Adding new services:**
   1. Define protocol in `Domain/Protocols/`
   2. Implement real service in `Data/Services/`
   3. Implement mock service in `Domain/Mocks/`
   4. Add factory method to `ServiceFactory`
   5. Update `MindsetApp.swift` to use factory
 - See `Context.md` §17 and `docs/guides/SERVICE_FACTORY_GUIDE.md` for complete documentation.

 
 # Scoping (for complex changes)
  - Prefer one package or one use case/flow per Composer request. For multi-file edits, name the target files or modules so changes stay predictable and reviewable.


  # Code Structure
  - Use Swift's latest features and protocol-oriented programming
  - Prefer value types (structs) over classes
  - Use MVVM architecture with SwiftUI
  - Structure: Features/, Core/, UI/, Resources/
  - Follow Apple's Human Interface Guidelines
  - **ViewModels stay clean:** ViewModels hold state and business logic only. Do not use HapticManager, UI types (Color, Font, View), or animation APIs in ViewModels—Views own haptics, animations, and visual feedback. ViewModels must not import SharedUtils for HapticManager; only Views call HapticManager when handling user actions or observing state.
  - **State in ViewModels:** Keep published/observable state (e.g. @State-backed, @Observable) in the ViewModel when possible. Use View @State only for state that would leak unnecessary framework or implementation details into the ViewModel (e.g. @FocusState, SwiftUI-only types, or one-off layout keys). Prefer a single source of truth in the ViewModel so logic and tests stay in one place.

  
  # Naming
  - camelCase for vars/funcs, PascalCase for types
  - Verbs for methods (fetchData)
  - Boolean: use is/has/should prefixes
  - Clear, descriptive names following Apple style
  - Data layer types that use the SwiftData framework use the `SD` prefix (e.g. `SDMindsetEntry`, `SDMindsetRepository`, `SDPersistenceService`). Domain and Feature modules do not use this prefix.
  - New screens/flows: add a Feature module, then register in `FeatureNavigation` (MainCoordinator) and app `AppViewFactory`; never import one Feature into another.
 
 
  # Swift Best Practices
  - Strong type system, proper optionals
  - async/await for concurrency
  - Result type for errors
  - @Published, @StateObject for state
  - Prefer let over var
  - Protocol extensions for shared code


 # Design System (SharedUI)
 - **Colors:** Always use `MindsetColors` from SharedUI. Never use raw `Color.orange`, `Color.green`, `.white`, etc. Use semantic tokens: `backgroundGrouped`, `textPrimaryAdaptive`, `accentOrange`, `successGreen`, `stoicSlateSoft`, `achievementGold`, etc.
 - **Fonts:** Always use `MindsetFonts` from SharedUI. Never use raw `.font(.headline)` or `.font(.caption)`. Use semantic tokens: `displayHeadline`, `promptHeadline`, `body`, `label`, `button`, etc.
 - **Layout:** Always use `MindsetLayout` from SharedUI for spacing, padding, corner radius, and dimensions. Never use magic numbers (e.g. `spacing: 12`, `.padding(25)`, `cornerRadius: 16`). Use tokens: `MindsetLayout.spacing12`, `MindsetLayout.paddingCard`, `MindsetLayout.radiusCard`, `MindsetLayout.buttonHeight`, etc.
 - **Sizing:** Prefer views and controls that size to their content. Avoid static or max widths/heights (e.g. `frame(maxWidth: _, maxHeight: 200)`) unless necessary—e.g. to prevent overflow, enforce a tap target (minHeight 44), or cap width for readability. Let height follow content; use `frame(maxWidth:)` only when needed for layout.
 - Feature modules must add SharedUI as a dependency to use MindsetColors, MindsetFonts, and MindsetLayout.
 - **Modals:** Use `DismissButton(action: { ... })` from SharedUI to dismiss modals and full-screen flows (onboarding, ritual, etc.). Do not build custom close buttons for these; use the shared component for consistency.
 - **Haptics:** Use `HapticManager` (SharedUtils) only in Views—never in ViewModels. Call the semantic APIs (selection(), action(), success(), tick()) from View code when handling taps or when observing state (e.g. success when isAiThinking goes false).
 - See `Packages/SharedUI/Sources/SharedUI/MindsetColors.swift`, `MindsetFonts.swift`, and `MindsetLayout.swift` for full token lists. See `Context.md` §13 for usage summary.

 
 # Localization (String Management)
 - **NEVER use hardcoded strings or magic strings** like `Text("Cancel")` or `String(localized: "ritual.title")`. Always use the type-safe localization APIs.
 - **SharedLocalization** for common strings used across multiple features:
   - Actions: `SharedLocalizedString.cancel`, `.save`, `.done`, `.back`, `.close`, `.continue`, `.retry`
   - Errors: `SharedLocalizedString.Error.networkError`, `.somethingWentWrong`, `.saveFailed`, `.tryAgain`
   - Validation: `SharedLocalizedString.Validation.requiredField`, `.invalidEmail`, `.tooShort`, `.tooLong`
   - Auth: `SharedLocalizedString.Auth.signIn`, `.signOut`, `.account`, `.profile`
   - Loading: `SharedLocalizedString.Loading.loading`, `.pleaseWait`, `.processing`
   - General: `SharedLocalizedString.General.settings`, `.help`, `.about`, `.feedback`
 - **Feature[Name]Strings** for feature-specific content (each Feature module has its own):
   - FeatureAuth: `FeatureAuthStrings.signInTitle`, `.signInWithApple`, `.Error.signInFailed`
   - FeatureDashboard: `FeatureDashboardStrings.Greeting.morning`, `.Streak.title`, `.TodayRitual.completed`
   - FeatureMindset: `FeatureMindsetStrings.title`, `.Categories.gratitude`, `.Success.title`
   - FeatureOnboarding: `FeatureOnboardingStrings.Welcome.title`, `.Pain.overwhelmedTitle`
   - FeatureSubscription: `FeatureSubscriptionStrings.Feature.aiCoach`, `.Plan.yearlyTitle`
   - FeatureHistory: `FeatureHistoryStrings.Empty.title`, `.Filter.week`
   - FeatureUserProfile: `FeatureUserProfileStrings.Stats.totalRituals`, `.Subscription.active`
 - **Decision tree:** Is the string used in multiple features? → Use `SharedLocalizedString`. Is it unique to one feature? → Use `Feature[Name]Strings`.
 - **Examples:**
   ```swift
   // ✅ GOOD - Type-safe, compile-time checked
   Text(SharedLocalizedString.cancel)
   Text(FeatureDashboardStrings.Greeting.morning)
   Button(FeatureAuthStrings.signInWithApple) { /* ... */ }
   errorMessage = SharedLocalizedString.Error.networkError
   
   // ❌ BAD - Magic strings, runtime errors
   Text("Cancel")  // Hardcoded
   Text(String(localized: "dashboard.greeting.morning"))  // Magic string
   ```
 - **Import statement:** Add `import SharedLocalization` when using common strings. Feature[Name]Strings are automatically available in their respective feature modules.
 - **Adding new strings:** Add to the appropriate `.xcstrings` file, then add the type-safe property to `SharedLocalization.swift` or `Feature[Name]Strings.swift`.
 - **See:** `docs/guides/LOCALIZATION_QUICK_REFERENCE.md` for full API reference and examples.

  # UI Development
  - SwiftUI first, UIKit when needed
  - SF Symbols for icons
  - Support dark mode, dynamic type
  - SafeArea and GeometryReader for layout
  - Handle all screen sizes and orientations
  - Implement proper keyboard handling
  - Implement Apple's "Liquid Glass" for existing and new UI https://developer.apple.com/documentation/TechnologyOverviews/liquid-glass
  - Always use Swift 6 / Swift 2026 idioms.
  - Prefer `@Observable` over `ObservableObject`.
  - Use `Group` or `Section` in SwiftUI to avoid deeply nested view hierarchies.
  - **View body composition:** Keep the main View struct minimal: only stored properties, `init`, and `body`. Put all subview implementations in a **private extension** on the View (e.g. `private extension DashboardView`). In the main struct, use `// MARK: - Body Composition` above `body`; in the extension, use `// MARK: - Subviews`. Extract sections like headers, content areas, overlays, and footers into named computed properties or functions in that extension (e.g. `headerSection`, `contentSection`, `footerOverlay`). The `body` should be a single ZStack/VStack that references these subviews. Improves readability, navigation, and maintainability.
  - When creating new Views, include:
    ```swift
    #if DEBUG
    @ObserveInjection var inject
    #endif
    // ... at end of body:
    .enableInjection()
    ```


