 # Logging & Debugging
 - **NEVER use print() in production code** — print statements are an anti-pattern: blocking I/O, can't be disabled, leak sensitive data, only visible in Xcode console.
 - **Use DebugLogger** (`SharedUtils`) for all app-level logging:
   - `DebugLogger.shared.add("✅ Success message")` — visible in UI debug overlay, persistent, async, non-blocking
   - Use emoji prefixes: ✅ success, ❌ errors, ⚠️ warnings, 📱 events, 🔄 state changes
   - Use `.localizedDescription` for errors (don't leak internal details)
   - Examples: auth callbacks, user sign-in/out, feature flow completion, critical errors
 - **When DebugLogger is appropriate:**
   - Auth/navigation lifecycle events (sign in, sign out, flow transitions)
   - Feature completion (ritual saved, onboarding done)
   - Critical errors that affect user experience
   - State changes useful for debugging in the field (via debug overlay)
 - **When print() is OK:**
   - Mock/test code only (e.g. MockAuthService)
   - Never in production ViewModels, Views, Services, or Repositories
 - **Cost vs Benefit:**
   - DebugLogger overhead: ~0.1ms per call (negligible)
   - Benefit: UI visibility, persistence, can take screenshots, helps remote debugging
   - Security: Sanitize sensitive data (e.g. URLs, tokens) before logging


